✅✅ Q1 : Explain about your test framework
🔹 1. Syzygy is a python based test automation framework used to F5OS applaince devices.
🔹 2. A test is implemented by a class derived from Testcase --> mornally it containts setup(), run() , teardown()
🔹 3. A test declares a equipment, services (network, console, labels, platfrom)
🔹 4. The equipment manager resolves the hardware and and injects it into the test trhough constructor EquipmentInstance()
🔹 5. Using equipment services like mgmt, power, console, labels we can able to SSH/Telnet to the hardware and execute the commands.
🔹 6. testtool.py runner loads the configuration , discover tests, resolves equipment, run the lead and saves the tests results in session summary
🔹 7. Syzygy has a extentions to save the tests session results to database
🔹 8. Extensions subscribe to framework events to provide logging, database storage, metrics, email notifications, triggers, and artifact handling.
This architecture separates test logic, equipment access, execution control, result reporting, and integrations, making the framework reusable and scalable.


✅✅ Q2 : Explain how you can design a test framework
🔹 1. I would design a test framework with clear layers for test cases, execution, equipment access, reporting, and configuration.
🔹 2.Each test would extend a common base class with a predictable lifecycle such as setup, run, and teardown.
🔹 3. I would use dependency injection so tests receive required services like network, console, database, or REST clients without creating them directly.
🔹 4. Test suites would be defined in YAML or JSON to control test selection, ordering, repetition, variables, and failure policies.
🔹 4. A central runner would discover tests, load configuration, resolve dependencies, execute tests, and produce a summary.
🔹 5. The framework would use structured results such as pass, failure, error, warning, and inconclusive instead of relying only on assertions.
🔹 6.Reusable conditions and actions would handle comparisons, waiting, retries, cancellation, and session aborts.
🔹 7.An event system would allow plugins for logging, database storage, metrics, notifications, artifact collection, and reporting.
🔹 8.The framework would support local and remote execution while keeping product-specific communication inside separate client or adapter modules.
🔹 9.Finally, I would validate the framework with unit, integration, and contract tests so it remains reliable, maintainable, and scalable.
